---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About | GeorgiaScience.org"
  description="Who we are and what we work toward: strengthening scientific literacy across Georgia through science education that has integrity."
>
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">About us</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Citizens, teachers, and scientists for a scientifically literate Georgia.</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      Georgia Citizens for Integrity in Science Education is a group of private citizens,
      teachers, and scientists working to improve science literacy across the state.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap content">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What we are</h2>
      <p>
        GeorgiaScience.org is the organization's public hub for science education,
        scientific literacy, and critical thinking. We provide clear, evidence-based
        resources for students, educators, and communities who value reliable science
        and informed decision making.
      </p>

      <h2>Our mission</h2>
      <p>
        Our mission is to strengthen scientific understanding, support high-quality STEM
        education, and help Georgians engage thoughtfully with evidence and with the world
        around them.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are literacy-forward. Scientific literacy, the ability to evaluate evidence,
        reason carefully, and make informed decisions, is the outcome we work toward.
        Integrity in science education is one of the means by which that literacy is built.
      </p>
      <p>
        By integrity in science education, we mean fidelity in how science is taught: that
        what reaches students and the public is accurate, current, evidence-based, and free
        from distortion. This is not about policing science. It is about teaching it well
        and honestly, so that genuine understanding can follow. A scientifically literate
        Georgia depends on science education that has integrity.
      </p>

      <h2>Who we serve</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Students seeking clarification on scientific topics and reliable resources.</li>
        <li>Educators looking for high-quality, vetted classroom materials and STEM support.</li>
        <li>Communities and individuals who want trustworthy information, upcoming events, and ways to engage thoughtfully with science in Georgia.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What belongs here</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Curated links to reputable national science and science-education organizations, selected for relevance to Georgia.</li>
        <li>Georgia-specific resources: state standards, local institutions, programs, and initiatives.</li>
        <li>Local science news: scholarships, discoveries, events, and policies affecting science education across Georgia.</li>
        <li>Plain-language explainers and critical-thinking and media-literacy resources.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What does not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Partisan political advocacy unrelated to science education.</li>
        <li>Unvetted or low-quality sources. Every linked resource should meet the same evidence and integrity standard we promote.</li>
        <li>Content that overstates certainty or blurs evidence with speculation.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where this came from</h2>
      <p>
        Georgia Citizens for Integrity in Science Education was founded in response to the
        warning stickers placed on biology textbooks in Cobb County in 2002, which described
        evolution as a theory rather than a fact. The organization described itself at the
        time as parents, clergy members, K-12 faculty, higher education faculty, and other
        concerned citizens, and it stated its purpose as promoting scientific literacy and
        excellence in science education. That is the same purpose this site works toward
        today.
      </p>
      <p>
        GCISE was among the organizations that joined the amicus curiae brief in
        <em>Selman v. Cobb County School District</em>, the federal case that ended with the
        stickers removed from Cobb County textbooks. The organization also described having
        worked to return the word evolution to the state science curriculum and to restore
        concepts including the age of the Earth, plate tectonics, and the Big Bang. That
        second account comes from GCISE's own description of its work rather than from an
        independent source, and we report it as such.
      </p>
      <p>
        The organization's activity thinned through the early 2010s and its website went
        quiet after 2013. The domain and the work later passed to the current curator. This
        site is a continuation of that effort rather than a new organization taking an old
        name.
      </p>

      <h2>The people who built it</h2>
      <p>
        Credit belongs to people who did the work long before this site existed. The
        following is drawn from GCISE's own published pages and from what these individuals
        have said publicly about their own roles.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Sarah Pallas, Ph.D.</strong> Co-founder, member, and the organization's
          webmaster, and a member of its Media Advisory Panel. She was Professor of
          Neuroscience and Biology at Georgia State University during her involvement and is
          now on the faculty at the University of Massachusetts Amherst.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Mark Farmer, Ph.D.</strong> Member and Media Advisory Panel member, then
          Professor and Chair of Cell Biology at the University of Georgia.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Jeffrey Selman.</strong> Lead plaintiff in <em>Selman v. Cobb County
          School District</em> and, by his own account, a member of the GCISE advisory board.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Carlos Moreno, Ph.D., Wes McCoy, Ph.D., and Ron Matson, Ph.D.</strong>
          Chair, Vice-Chair, and Secretary respectively, as listed on the organization's
          site in 2013.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Many others contributed as members, as teachers, and as citizens who showed up. The
        record we have is partial, and we would rather say so than imply it is complete. If
        you were involved and we have your role wrong or missing, please tell us.
      </p>

      <h2>Who curates this site</h2>
      <div class="curator">
        <img
          src="/curator-finley.jpg"
          alt="Reginald Finley, curator of GeorgiaScience.org"
          class="curator-photo"
          width="560"
          height="560"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <div class="curator-id">
          <p class="curator-name">Reginald Finley, Ph.D.</p>
          <p class="curator-role">Curator</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        GeorgiaScience.org is curated by Reginald Finley, Ph.D., who teaches biology, human
        anatomy and physiology, health sciences, and scientific literacy as adjunct faculty
        at Franklin University.
      </p>
      <p>
        What drew him to this work was health misinformation: watching accurate information
        about the human body lose ground to confident claims that were simply easier to
        repeat. Generative AI has since made that problem faster and more fluent, which is
        the subject of his book <a
          href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0H4LSJGD9"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        ><em>AI-Epistemic Resilience: A Framework for Knowledge Integrity in the Age of
        Artificial Intelligence</em></a> (2026). He also founded the <a
          href="https://accesstorobotics.org"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Finley Robotics Initiative</a>, a nonprofit expanding AI and robotics access for
        underserved students. He lives in Newnan, Georgia.
      </p>
      <p>
        Curator is the accurate word for the job. This site does not conduct original
        research or issue findings. It selects, verifies, and organizes the work of others,
        and it says where everything came from so readers can check.
      </p>

      <div class="principle">
        <p>
          We try to practice the literacy we teach: verify claims, cite sources, state
          uncertainty honestly, and distinguish evidence from interpretation. Our
          credibility rests on holding ourselves to our own standards.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-head { padding-block: clamp(1.75rem, 1.25rem + 2vw, 2.75rem) 1rem; }
  .eyebrow { text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; font-size: var(--step--1); font-weight: 600; color: var(--deep); margin: 0 0 0.6rem; }
  .page-title { font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: var(--step-3); line-height: 1.1; margin: 0 0 1rem; max-width: 26ch; }
  .page-intro { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.55; margin: 0; max-width: 76ch; }

  .content { padding-bottom: 4rem; }
  .prose { max-width: min(88ch, 100%); margin-inline: 0; }
  .prose h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: var(--step-2);
    margin: 2.25rem 0 0.75rem; line-height: 1.15;
  }
  .prose p { font-size: var(--step-0); line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }
  .prose ul { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; padding-left: 1.25rem; }
  .prose li { font-size: var(--step-0); line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink); margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }

  .curator {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .curator-photo {
    width: 108px;
    height: 108px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    object-fit: cover;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border: 3px solid var(--surface);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--hairline);
  }
  .curator-id { min-width: 0; }
  .curator-name {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    margin: 0 0 0.15rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  .curator-role {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .principle {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.75rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
  }
  .principle p { margin: 0; font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink); }
</style>
